brock@cs.unca.edu (J. Dean Brock) writes:

>Our NFS server runs Digital OSF/1 V3.0.  We have clients
>running Ultrix 4.4, Solaris 2.4, and Digital Unix 3.2C.
>The 3.2C clients were often slow and got a lot of
>	NFS server not responding messages
>When we updated one of our Sparc's to Solaris 2.5, we noticed
>that it also got slower.  Since we know that one of the
>enhancements in Solaris 2.5 is the update of NFS from version
>2.0 to 3.0, we wondered if NFS version 3.0 was the problem.
>We decided to test the theory by having the Alpha clients
>use NFS version 2.0.

BTW, SUN RPC doesn't have a .x concept in its protocols.  It is NFS V2
and V3.  Things are complicated a bit since we introduced NFS V3 in
Digital UNIX V3.0 (which was really called OSF/1...).

In general, the the only thing that tends to be slower with NFS V3
is directory reading, and then primarily things that don't require
the attributes of the files.  (E.g. filename completion.)  This is
due to the READDIRPLUS operation in V3 that does most of the work "ls -l"
does vs. READDIR, which does essentially "ls".  Almost everything else
should be nearly the same or much faster.

>However, when I modified /etc/vfstab on the Solaris 2.5
>client to make it use NFS version 2.0, our Digital Unix
>server paniced.  I guess there's a kernel bug out there.
>So, don't try this on machines running Solaris 2.5!

I'm aware of server problems handling the ACL sideband protocols from
Sun and IBM, but I thought they were limited to V3.2.  V3.2c and V3.2d
have fixes, there's a patch for V3.2.  In some cases, AIX mostly, the
server would lose memory (see vmstat -M)  That leads to slow servers
and not responding messages.

So, main things to look into:

1) Exactly what is slow about V3?  I would expect that "ls -l" and write
   be much faster.

2) Check server for lost memory.

3) Consider upgrading the server.

4) Try disabling the ACL stuff from Solaris.
