(Rob Hooft) writes:

|>Recently our institute acquired a number of new ALPHA stations. They
|>are running DigitalUnix 3.2c, and fortran compiler 3.8.0. If I compile
|>my software on these machines, even at "-O3" (let alone -O4 or -O5)
|>the program is highly unstable.  Even worse: the program crashes at
|>different points when it's run under the debugger or outside! I
|>tried to produce stack traces, but the only thing dbx tells me is
|>something like "cannot read memory at 0x1". The binary produced on my
|>own workstation runs absolutely without any problem....
|>
|>Is 3.8.0 the latest version of the compiler? Are there any known code
|>generation bugs, or am I running into weird never uncovered bugs of my
|>own code? I'm tempted to ask the system group not to upgrade the software
|>on my machine yet, but on the other hand the software must run at other
|>sites as well!

Yes, 3.8 is the current version.  The symptoms you are describing suggest
that your program is accessing uninitialized memory, or is corrupting
memory somewhere.  This can be a difficult problem to track down.  3.8
is, we feel, a very stable compiler, but we don't claim it to be bug-free.
That it does not fail when using the older compiler does not necessarily
mean the compiler is at fault - it may mean that a new optimization revealed
a hidden bug in your code. (Or it could be the compiler is in error.)

If you have a reproducible example of the problem, I'd be pleased to look
at it - send me mail.  You should also report problems through your local
Digital software support contact, as this is the way you would be able to
receive any updates.
