The part numbers are DEFTA-FA and DEFTA-AA.  The -FA has been discontinued and
the -AA is the replacement.  The only down side to this is that the -AA only
works under Alpha VMS v6.2 and up, and doesn't work under any version of VAX/VMS.
We bought a truckload of -AA's and had to trade them for used -FA's because we're
still running Alpha v1.5 and VAX v5.5-2H4 in several places.  Don't you just LOVE
the new DEC!

marketing: Hey what's this DEFTA-FA thingy?

engineering: It's a slick card that works on a reliable bus under any version
             of VMS released over the last three years.

marketing: You mean that people don't have to run our newest version to use this
           card?!?

engineering: (with a groan) Yes, it also means that we have some reliable drivers
             that have already been tested.

marketing: Forget that!!  The CSC isn't getting enough calls and there isn't
           enough demand for VMS v6.2!  Discontinue the card and design a new one
           that doesn't work with any previous version of the operating system.

marketing VP: Hmmm, perhaps we should confuse the issue even further.  Make sure
              that the DECdirect catalog has both part numbers at the same price,
              but don't make any distinction between them.  Additionally, don't
              let anyone in presales Tech, or the CSC know what the difference is.

engineering: Well, hell.  As long as you're at it, just ship the new -AA even if
             they order a -FA!

marketing VP: Brilliant.  Let's promote all of our good engineers into marketing.
              That way we'll have code that's so buggy, people won't have a choice
              but to buy a full support contract, and the CSC will be profitable
              again!

Brett

P.S.

