Chris Johnson (johnson@neu.edu) said:

> I'm trying to get an output filter to work from printcap for lpd.  
> Having trouble doing so.


Several others replied with schemes to get it to work, but none that were 
completely happy with their solutions.

I bought a DEClaser 5100 printer and made the assumption that Digital could help
me make it work with my Dunix system. Silly me. After quite a number of traded
messages with the CSC, I stumbled upon a solution that worked for me.

Here is what my printcap file looks like:
	prez|lp0|lp|0:\
	        :lp=/dev/nic/prez:\
	        :of=/usr/lbin/ps_level2of:\
	        :sd=/usr/spool/prez:

In my case, /dev/nic/prez is a pipe created by the following startup script:

	#!/sbin/sh
	#
	# Control the remote line-printers
	
	case "$1" in
	'start')
	        if /usr/nic/nic_print /dev/nic/prez prez 10001&; then
	                echo "Prez print service started"
	        fi
	        ;;
	*)
	        echo "usage: $0 {start}"
	        ;;
	esac

Since the 5100 uses a telnet type connection through port 10001, the only 
way for me to talk to it is through this pseudo device. I noticed that in your 
case, you have a similar sort of connection mechanism, so this may work for you.

The ps_level2of is capable of recognizing both text and PS format files, so I
just have the single queue.

I got misled as did you because I could get the command line form to work but
not the printcap. The use of the lp option seemed to be the trick.

Greg
