In article <4e3lrp$r7s@nntpd2.cxo.dec.com>, Alan Rollow (alan@nabeth.cxo.dec.com) wrote:


> The particular problem with Advfs and News is that the built-in
> assumptions about average file size are worthless since News
> files are smaller than assumed.  Left to itself the file system
> will dynamically allocate "inode" space (*) as new files are
> created.  It allocates this space in fairly large chunks which
> have to be contiguous.  If I recall correctly, two things can
> cause it to fail:

What I'd like to know is why this seemed to be such a stunning revelation
to Digital.

News spools have had pathological file system issues on every platform.

Digital Unix boxes were being sold into the Internet server market.

Internet servers tend to run news spools.

Thus, Digital Unix file systems should be able to easily run news spools, 
and, Digital Unix support should be able to deal with the problems that
come up.

This straightforward reasoning seemed utterly foreign to Digital when
we encountered these problems and brought them to Digital last spring.

	- Saul
