In article <BCD.96Jan10132303@heinlein.mps.ohio-state.edu>, bcd@pacific.mps.ohio-state.edu (Bryan Dunlap) writes:
#We are running OSF/1 1.3 and 3.2C on a number of Alphas, and trying to
#mount several filesystems from a VMS cluster which does NFS using TGV
#Multinet.  While the OSF hosts can mount the filesystems, they are not
#mapping UIDs properly -- everything is owned by user 'nobody' when
#viewed from OSF.  SunOS hosts and Ultrix hosts mounting the same
#filesystems map the UIDs properly (all the Unix hosts are in the
#exports file on VMS, and some program is run on VMS periodically using
#the Unix passwd file to map UIDs by username between the two
#environments -- all the Unix hosts are in the same yp domain).  

I suspect you're using NFS Groups on the MultiNet system, which may not be
setup correctly -- often the cause is a system which is listed with simply
its hostname in the NFS Groups setup, but the system is known (and advertises
itself) using its FQDN.

#I am
#using the following mount options:
#
#   rw,soft,intr,bg,retrans=8,timeo=20
#
#These options work fine on the other unixes, and I've tried some
#others but nothing changed the behavior.
#
#Does anyone know why this would happen?  I find it odd that OSF/1 is
#the only one that doesn't map the VMS userids.  It mounts filesystems
#from the other Unix variants just fine.  
#
#Please reply via email.

On the MultiNet system, you can run TCPDUMP to see what's happening between
the two systems:

  $ MULTINET TCPDUMP/SNAP=1514/RPC=ALL UDP AND HOST ip_addr

Where "ip_addr" is the IP Address of the OSF/1 machine.

If you have further questions, contact TGV technical support by calling 
408-457-5201, or send Email to MultiNet-VMS@support.tgv.com.

