In Article <4d5ld3$53n@csl2.csl-gmbh.net>
kluge@ave.ac.agit.de (Steffen Kluge) writes:
>In article <4d3rfd$2hbk@ausnews.austin.ibm.com>,
> <gthome@austin.ibm.com> wrote:
>>In article <30F5121B.4FDC@ccr.hydro.qc.ca>,
>>Alain Martineau  <Alain.Martineau@ccr.hydro.qc.ca> wrote:
>>>I would like to know how much the time on two nodes can be 
>>>synchronized using DCE. Let's say those are to alpha stations, 200 
>>>Mhz on a quiet (or switched) ethernet. 
>>
>>My RS/6000s on token ring are well within one second.  The 
>>Distributed Time Service keeps the system clocks sync'd
>>closely.
>
>We are using xntpd (version 2) and ntpd (version 1) on the hosts
>of our local ethernet and they never get more than 0.001 seconds
>out of sync during normal operation!

The basic ideas behind DCE's time service and NTP are very different. For
keeping two systems in sync, the DCE service is probably better, but for
syncing time to some external source (WWVB?), NTP is probably better.

One nice feature of the DCE service is that the maximum possible inaccuracy is
included with the time. The more systems you have running TSS on a LAN, the
smaller this number will be. It was specifically designed for situations where
timestamps on messages must be properly ordered between systems. The DCE Cell
Name Service is such a case.

Both NTP and the DCE time service will keep system time very accurate, but for
pure accuracy most of the time, NTP will win. For stability and proveable
system synchronization, NTP loses. For almost all cases, both are more than
adequate.

