>  Here is the printcap entry I'm using:
[snip]
>  This prints postscript and PCL. To get text as well, there are two
>  simple options, neither of which is entirely satisfactory:
>  1. Define another printcap entry to point to the text queue on the
>     JetDirect card:
[snip]
>     This gives you two separate queues with two printcap entries for
>     the printer, one for raw input (PCL, postscript, HPGL2), the other
>     for ASCII text.  You have to remember to use the right queue to
>     print the appropriate sort of file.
>  2. Locally run a filter to convert text to postscript or PCL as a
>     wrapper around your lpr command.  You can do this with a one-liner
>     along the lines of 
>       /usr/lbin/hplaserof < filename | lpr -Plp1
>  Phil

The other alternative that I have managed to get going (originally for
debugging purposes) was to create a print queue to which all jobs are
submitted. This print queue has an lp=/dev/null entry. Then, attach an
output filter on the queue that can decide whether the job is PS or text
(look for %!PS-Adobe in the first line) and optionally run the 
appropriate processing. The filter then submits the job to another queue
that has the appropriate rm and rp entries. It's not really elegant but
it means that you do effectively get an of on a remote printer.

Hope this helps, 

Boyd
