In article <4d3usa$gfm@nntpd2.cxo.dec.com>, spratte@odette.cxo.dec.com
(Michael Spratte) wrote:
...
| I've had no problem with the RFC1321 code running on Digital UNIX.  All
| I did was modify global.h:
| 
| /* UINT4 defines a four byte word */
| #if defined(__osf__) && defined(__alpha)
| typedef unsigned int UINT4;
| #else
| typedef unsigned long int UINT4;
| #endif
| 
| With this modification, the code works identically on ULTRIX, OpenVMS,
| Digital UNIX, and Windows NT.

That's about the same as what I proposed to the author of Python*, and
it turns out there was already a slightly more elegant solution based on
the symbols LONG_BIT and WORD_BIT, in sys/limits.h.  These symbols
evidently are intended to expose the size in bits of "long int" and "int"
respectively.  So the following works not only on the Alpha, but also on
64-bit MIPS hardware etc., as long as there's a sys/limits.h and it has these
symbols, which is true at least on several UNIX platforms.

#include <sys/limits.h>
#if WORD_BIT == 32
typedef unsigned int UINT4;
#else
# if LONG_BIT == 32
typedef unsigned long int UINT4;
# else
what have we here
# endif
#endif

    Donn Cave, University Computing Services, University of Washington
    donn@u.washington.edu

* Python is an interpreted language;  it incorporates this code in its MD5
   support module.  Direct your browser to http://www.python.org.



