From: claude@lovelace.infobiogen.fr (Claude Scarpelli)
Subject: Most shells don't handle SIGCHLD correctly
Date: 1996/01/16
Message-ID: <4dgcf4$hho@lovelace.infobiogen.fr>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 135377622
distribution: inet
content-type: text/plain; charset=iso-8859-1
organization: GIS INFOBIOGEN, 7 rue Guy Moquet BP8, 94801 VILLEJUIF, France
mime-version: 1.0
newsgroups: comp.unix.solaris,comp.unix.shell,comp.unix.osf.misc,comp.unix.osf.osf1

I just discovered some weird behaviour of most shell on most unix
system. here is a two lines test script:

lovelace$ cat tt 
sleep 1000
echo done....
lovelace$ 

I run this program with my favourite shell on my favorite unix system,
and I send the signal SIGSTOP to the 'sleep 1000' process.

lovelace$ sh tt&
[1] 16826
lovelace$ ps -fu claude | grep sleep
  claude 16834  4349  3 15:13:07 pts/6    0:00 grep sleep
  claude 16827 16826  3 15:12:56 pts/6    0:00 sleep 1000


I send SIGSTOP....


lovelace$ kill -STOP 16827
done....
lovelace$ 


The shell run the next line of the shell script ! 

Obviously, the shell receive the signal SIGCHLD because its child
changed its state (it is becoming stopped). 

I would say this is an incorrect behaviour : The shell should check
its child terminated or may be should call sigaction(SIGCHLD...) with
the SA_NOCLDSTOP flag.

Here is a small summary of system/shell which exhibit what I call a
bug, and those who seems to have the correct behaviour:

UNIX                           SHELL               BUGGY ?
-----------------------------------------------------------
SunOS 4.1.3		       /bin/sh		   No
SunOS 4.1.3		       Bash 1.12.1	   Yes
Solaris 2.4		       /bin/sh		   Yes
Solaris 2.4		       Bash 1.14.5(1)	   Yes
OSF1 V3.2		       /usr/ucb/sh	   No
OSF1 V3.2		       Bash 1.14.4(1)	   Yes

Can someone comment on that ?
-- 
