From: werme@alingo.zk3.dec.com (Eric Werme)
Subject: Re: DEC Alpha NFS serving perfs and disk I/O issues?
Date: 1996/01/16
Message-ID: <werme.821827458@alingo.zk3.dec.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 135532226
references: <4de5ov$g1t@nntp.atlanta.com>
organization: Digital Equipment Corporation
reply-to: werme@zk3.dec.com
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1,comp.protocols.nfs

curts@compgen.com (Curt Smith) writes:

>Is there any DEC docs or NFS in general that gives NFS server and client
>performances and system load modeling?

There's a USENIX paper on NFS V3, but it's no help to determine the
limits of any system.  It mostly compares V3 to V2 and looks at
the behaviour of writing large files and find performance, both areas
that V3 offers improvements.

Do you have the SPEC-SFS (LADDIS) data for our systems?  From other Email to
me it appears you're a distributor.  I don't have our official data handy,
but could help get it to you Thursday.  A shortcoming with SPEC-SFS is that
it only covers NFS V2, but SPEC is working on that.

>I would like to determine the CPU load of the NFS server and client for 
>a given file I/O load and then compare that to same file load for to 
>a local disk?

Now, LADDIS is only a server benchmark, there are no good metrics of client
throughput, at least nothing that is good for competitive purposes or
system sizing.  As far as I know, that's a industry-wide problem.

If I mount a MFS (Memory File System), I can nearly saturate a FDDI wire
with a single threaded application writing to a file.  (9 MByte/sec UDP,
8.5 TCP.)  With writing to a striped disk, we've seen 6 Mbyte/sec.  That
was with a pair of 175 Mhz Alphas, haven't had a chance to try faster
machines yet.

For a mix that does mostly directory operations, then you have a very
different job mix and disk seek times become important on file systems
like UFS.  One nice thing about SPEC-SFS is that the mix is adjustable,
so one has a chance to emulating a load before actually having to deal
with it in a production environment.

Unfortunately, the whole issue of sizing a NFS system is simply a
difficult problem.
