In article <4cd9i6$p4i$2@mhafn.production.compuserve.com>, Greg Mays <71612.3473@CompuServe.COM> writes:
>All:
>
>I've been exploring an obscure option of the dcp (DECNET Copy) 
>command and can't find some answers I need.  Specifically I've
>been experimenting with the dcp -s command which submits remote
>output files for execution.  The command works ok but lacks control.
>I can't see anyway to control the batch queue or other execution 
>options of the job on the remote system.  
>
>I have been able to use dcp -s to submit a DCL procedure on a 
>remote VAX system but can't control what queue processes the job
>(/QUEUE qualifier for a VAX job) or the parameters passed to the
>job (/PARAM qualifier for a VAX job).
>
>Is there anyway to exert more control over the remote job
>characteristics??
>

Hmm, the only dcp command I know of is part of Ki Research's KiNet
implementation of DECnet Phase IV.

In any case, you have no direct control of queues when doing remote submits via
DECnet.  You can prevent printout of the log file of the job you are starting
by assigning sys$print to nla0: in that command file.  You can use two command
files, the first simply using the submit command to start the second as
desired.

Possibly FAL.COM on the recieving system could be modified to assign sys$batch
and/or sys$print for you under special, detectable circumstances.

