Of course CDE is more than the window manager.  One reason I'm
interested in it is that our users have never quite figured out how to
manipulate .Xdefaults and .xinitrc.  CDE lets you do a reasonable job
of customizing your enviornment using a GUI tool.  It will also save
the state of your session and put it back when you log in again.  This
isn't perfect -- I can do a better job of customizing my environment
by editing configuration files -- but a lot of our users would see it
as a big improvement.

It also seem to have the necessary tools to let you build a GUI
environment for normal usage.  It would require some work on the part
of the support staff, to understand how to set up printers, how to add
new file types to the file manager, how to customize the bar at the
bottom, etc.  But for sites who would like to make life easier for
relatively unsophisticated users, I think CDE is a reasonable start.

However at least with 3.2C I recommend at least 48 MB of memory for
using it.  Unfortunately most of our systems have only 32 MB.  The
difference between 32 and 48 with CDE is dramatic.  I'm qualifying
this carefully by saying "with 3.2C".  DEC has announced that they are
going to make CDE usable on a 24 MB system under 4.0.






