X-Deja-AN: 133929937
distribution: world
references: <4cd9i6$p4i$2@mhafn.production.compuserve.com>
organization: Technology Strategy and Architecture
reply-to: kaiser@acm.org
newsgroups: cern.alpha,comp.unix.osf.osf1,comp.unix.ultrix,vmsnet.networks.tcp-ip.ucx


In article <4cd9i6$p4i$2@mhafn.production.compuserve.com>, Greg Mays
<71612.3473@CompuServe.COM> writes:

> Is there anyway to exert [detailed] control over the remote job
> characteristics [of jobs submitted with "dcp -s"]?

Not that I know of.  My own approach, which works for my simple needs, is
twofold:

    1.	For common things, set up command procedures on the VMS side that
	start batch jobs with the proper queue parameters, then use "dcp -s"
	on the UNIX side to submit a job that invokes one of those procedures.

    2.	Let software on the UNIX side write a file which, when submitted as
	a job on the VMS side, writes a procedure with the parameters I want,
	and then submits that.

This indirect technique works for me, but it'd probably be a nightmare for
any large number of users or any very wide variety of queue options.
Remember that no two VMS systems have identical collections of queues and
parameters!  That's why there are packages around to handle details of
queuing between UNIX and VMS.

